Introduction
Two bars can share the same open, high, low, and close and still tell opposite stories. In one, buyers lifted the offer again and again until the price drifted up. In the other, sellers hit the bid all the way down, and a few quote jumps carried the price up anyway. OHLC data cannot separate the two, because it records where price went and not how it got there.
Order flow delta is one of the simplest tools for reading that hidden story. It subtracts seller initiated volume from buyer initiated volume inside each bar. On trade data, a strongly positive value suggests aggressive buying, a strongly negative value suggests aggressive selling, and a value near zero suggests balance. On quote data, the same values describe quote pressure only. Computing it requires raw ticks because only ticks carry the direction of price changes inside a bar.
This article presents an indicator that computes delta from raw ticks. For each bar, it reads ticks via CopyTicksRange(), classifies them with the tick rule, and aggregates buy, sell, and unclassified volume (or tick counts in proxy mode). It plots per-bar or cumulative delta as a colored histogram and marks divergences with arrows.
One caveat belongs at the start. Most feeds do not report who initiated a trade, so the tick rule infers the answer from price changes. The result is a context signal for judging pressure. It is not a record of what happened, and it is not a forecast of what comes next.
The project consists of small, pure, testable classes plus a thin wrapper over the terminal tick history. The article explains how to carry state across bars, avoid plotting missing data as zero, and recalculate only changed parts. It introduces the theory first, then reviews each class, and ends with tests, annotated screenshots, and a list of limitations.
Order Flow Delta and the Tick Rule
This section defines the quantities the code computes and the assumptions behind them. Every design decision later in the article traces back to one of these definitions, so it is worth reading them closely.
What delta measures
Buyer initiated volume is volume where the buyer was the aggressor, meaning the trade printed at or above the ask. Seller initiated volume is the mirror case. Delta is buyer initiated volume minus seller initiated volume for a bar.
A positive delta suggests net buying pressure inside the bar, and a negative delta suggests net selling pressure. Delta can disagree with the candle. A bar can close higher while most of its classified volume was seller initiated. The close reflects where the last trades printed. Delta reflects how the volume was split along the way. These definitions apply to trade-based data. In the quote-based mode, buyer initiated means only that the bid moved up.
The tick rule
The tick rule compares each tick price with the previous tick price from the same source:
A price above the previous price is a buy.
A price below the previous price is a sell.
An unchanged price repeats the most recent direction.
An unchanged price carries no new information about who initiated the trade, so the rule keeps the latest evidence. Without that step, long runs of equal prices would stay unclassified. A tick that still cannot be classified goes into its own unclassified bucket. That covers the first tick the indicator sees, and an unchanged tick when no direction has been established yet.
The rule is a standard approximation, not a measurement. It can misclassify trades that occur at an unchanged price. Classification from bid quotes is also weaker than classification from trade prices, because quotes can move without any trade.
The Bid source has one more weakness. A quote update that changes only the ask arrives as a tick with an unchanged bid, so the rule repeats the previous direction. A long run of ask-only updates therefore piles up on the side of the last bid move, with no trade and no bid change behind it.
MqlTick
can carry the TICK_FLAG_BUY and TICK_FLAG_SELL flags on some feeds. This project uses the tick rule because those flags are not available on every feed. Flag-based classification is covered later as an extension.
Price sources and volume modes
The price source is either the Last price or the Bid price. The volume mode is either the tick volume field of each MqlTick or a tick count of one per tick. The tick volume field is meaningful on exchange symbols with Last-price ticks, where each trade carries a volume.
The terminal also reports a tick volume for each bar, which is the number of ticks in that bar. This article calls it the bar tick volume, to keep it distinct from the tick volume field of a single tick.
On symbols such as spot forex, trade ticks and volume fields are usually empty. The Bid source with tick count mode is then the working combination, and it is a proxy: it counts quote updates, not traded volume. The sub-window title says so on the chart. The Bid source combined with the tick volume field normally gives an all-zero delta, so
OnInit()
prints a warning for that pairing.
Two modes, two meanings
The two supported combinations measure different things, and this article keeps them apart.
The trade-based mode uses the Last price with the tick volume field. It approximates order flow delta in the usual sense, with the tick rule standing in for the unknown aggressor.
The quote-based mode uses the Bid price with tick count. It counts bid updates that moved up or down, so it describes quote pressure, not traded volume. The article calls its output the quote update proxy.
Architecture
Figure 1 shows the whole project on one page. Data flows down the spine from the indicator to the divergence detector, and then splits into the two plots. The two side boxes are the terminal tick history on the left and the shared types on the right.
Architectural Diagram
Figure 1: Architecture of the project: The indicator calls the reader, which wraps CopyTicksRange(). Ticks flow through the classifier into the aggregator, and the detector consumes the cumulative series. The histogram and the divergence arrows are the two outputs. The dashed arrow marks a shared dependency, not a data flow.
The Data Model: OrderFlowTypes.mqh
Every other file depends on this one, so it comes first. It defines the vocabulary of the project: five enums, three color index constants, and two data structures. The central rule of the data model is simple. Any value that is not computed must be explicitly marked as undefined. Every figure that can be undefined travels with a flag, and the plot buffers hold EMPTY_VALUE for undefined bars, so the chart never shows a missing value as zero. Internal fields such as delta, total_volume, and the cumulative buffer do hold 0.0 for an undefined bar, so code that reads them must check the matching flag.
Every enum and constant carries an OF_ or ENUM_OF_ prefix, so nothing collides with built-in names such as TICK_FLAG_BUY. The comment after each enum value becomes its label in the inputs dialog. Direction has an explicit OF_DIR_NONE value, so an unclassified tick is never confused with a buy or a sell.
