Reading Tick Data per Bar: TickDataReader.mqh
Everything above this file is pure arithmetic. This file is the only place where the program asks the terminal for data, which makes it the only place where data can be missing, late, or wrong. Isolating that risk in one small class keeps the rest of the project testable and makes failure handling explicit.
The class does two jobs. It converts bar times into exact millisecond boundaries, and it wraps CopyTicksRange() so that a failure is reported instead of hidden.
Millisecond boundaries
CopyTicksRange() takes inclusive millisecond bounds. A bar covers the range from its open time multiplied by 1000, to the next bar open time multiplied by 1000 minus 1. The next bar then starts exactly one millisecond later, so every tick belongs to one bar, with no overlap and no gap.
BuildRange() is separate from the terminal call so the arithmetic can be tested without tick history. It rejects empty and reversed ranges. ChooseFlags() maps the price source to copy flags: the Last source needs trade ticks, and the Bid source needs quote-change ticks.
