ClassifyDirection() applies the tick rule to one price change. With no previous price, it returns none. An unchanged price has its own explicit branch and repeats the previous direction, so a zero difference is never treated as a buy or a sell. Otherwise, a rise is a buy and a fall is a sell.
OF_PRICE_EPSILON is far smaller than any real tick size, so only prices that are identical in practice count as unchanged.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| ClassifyDirection                                                |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
ENUM_OF_DIRECTION CTickClassifier::ClassifyDirection(
const
bool
has_previous,
const
double
previous_price,
const
ENUM_OF_DIRECTION previous_direction,
const
double
new_price)
const
{
//--- no earlier price: the tick cannot be classified
if
(!has_previous)
return
(OF_DIR_NONE);
//--- price difference against the previous tick
double
difference=new_price-previous_price;
//--- unchanged price: repeat the previous direction (zero is never a buy or a sell by itself)
if
(::
MathAbs
(difference)<=OF_PRICE_EPSILON)
return
(previous_direction);
//--- price increase is a buy, price decrease is a sell
if
(difference>
0.0
)
return
(OF_DIR_BUY);
return
(OF_DIR_SELL);
  }
