Cumulative Delta and Divergence Theory
Before looking at the detector, we need an exact definition of divergence. A vague definition would produce a vague detector and tests that prove nothing. This section states the rule, its edge cases, and the reason signals are evaluated on closed bars only.
Per-bar delta tells us who won one bar. Cumulative delta is the running sum of those bars, so it shows whether pressure is building or fading across many bars. It can reveal that a rising market is met with steadily weaker upward pressure.
The divergence rule is exact: for bar i, find the bar j with the highest high in the preceding lookback window (excluding i ). A bearish divergence occurs when high(i) > high(j) and cumulative(i) < cumulative(j). The bullish case is symmetric. The low of bar i is strictly lower than the lowest low in the window, while cumulative delta is strictly higher than at that bar.
Equal highs, equal lows, or equal cumulative delta values are not divergences. Window bars with undefined cumulative delta are skipped. If several window bars share the extreme price, the most recent one is the reference. Bearish takes precedence if both conditions hold on the same bar.
With the daily reset on, a window that spans a server midnight would compare totals with different baselines. The indicator skips the divergence check for those bars, so the first lookback bars of each day never produce a signal. Because the rule compares two points of the same series, a constant offset in the cumulative base does not change the result. Only a reset or a data hole inside the window can.
Closed bars only
Divergence is evaluated on closed bars only. The forming bar has a changing high, low, and delta, so a signal on it can appear and vanish within seconds. Waiting for the close costs one bar of latency and removes that flicker, which is a deliberate design choice.
A closed-bar signal is stable once the tick history for its window is complete. No arrow is drawn while a window contains a missing bar. If a retry later fills the bar, signals near that bar can appear late.
The Divergence Detector: DivergenceDetector.mqh
The detector implements the rule above as a pure function. It receives arrays and indexes, returns an enum, and never touches the chart. Its main job is to say none whenever the evidence is missing, equal, or too short.
DetectAtBar() takes chronological high, low, and cumulative series, the defined flags, a bar index, and a lookback length. It validates the request first. A lookback below one, a bad index, or a window that would start before bar zero returns none, and so does an undefined cumulative value at the signal bar. The window scan then skips undefined bars and resolves ties to the most recent bar. The final comparisons are strict.
