Building the Indicator: OrderFlowDelta.mq5
The indicator is the only file that touches the chart. It connects the classes, owns the buffers, and decides what to recompute on each call. This section follows the order in which the terminal runs the code: declarations, initialization, calculation, and shutdown.
Display and buffer decisions
The histogram plots whichever series the display mode selects, either per-bar delta or cumulative delta. Its color always follows the sign of the per-bar delta of the same bar, in both modes. Divergence detection always uses cumulative delta, regardless of display mode.
All buffers and helper arrays are indexed chronologically, with index 0 as the oldest bar. OnInit() calls ArraySetAsSeries(false) on each of them, and OnCalculate() sets the same indexing on its input arrays explicitly, so the code does not depend on a default.
There are 8 buffers and 3 plots. Buffers 0 and 1 are the color histogram, holding values and color indexes. Buffer 2 is the bearish arrow, and buffer 3 is the bullish arrow. Buffers 4 to 7 are calculation buffers: per-bar delta, running cumulative delta, and the end-of-bar price and direction that make incremental recalculation possible.
The defaults use Bid + tick count because most symbols provide quote ticks. However, this is the weakest mode for traded-pressure analysis; use Last + tick volume whenever trade ticks are available. The first calculation processes up to InpMaxBars bars, from 10 to 100000, and each bar costs one CopyTicksRange() call. The window does not slide afterward. New bars extend it until the next full calculation, which happens on a reload, a timeframe change, or a history refresh.
The numeric defaults are safe starting values, not tuned results. InpMaxBars is 500 because each bar costs one CopyTicksRange() call, and 500 calls load quickly while still covering roughly three weeks of H1 bars. On a slow connection or an empty tick cache, lower it. On H4 or D1, 500 bars reach back further and are usually enough. InpDivLookback is 20 because it gives a window long enough to hold a clear swing and short enough to compare nearby highs and lows. Shorter windows give more signals and more noise. Longer windows give fewer signals that arrive later. A retry runs at most every 10 seconds, because tick history normally needs a few seconds to synchronize and shorter pauses only repeat the same failing call. The six retries therefore span at least one minute on an active symbol, which is long enough for a normal synchronization and short enough to stop a symbol without the selected ticks from being read again and again. For thin symbols, raise the retry interval. For very active symbols, keep InpMaxBars low, because the forming bar is re-read in full on every call.
Two details affect how the chart reads. The color shows the sign of the per-bar delta, so a green bar in cumulative mode does not mean that the total is positive. Divergence arrows sit at the plotted value of their bar, so in per-bar mode their height says nothing about the cumulative values that triggered them.
